<div class="tldr"><p><strong>Short answer:</strong> the best women's ski jacket fits over your layers without being boxy, is waterproof and breathable enough for where you ski, and matches how cold you run: insulated if you get cold easily or ski in very cold places, a shell if you want to layer for changing conditions. Look for a powder skirt, pit zips, a helmet-compatible hood, and enough length to cover your waist when you bend.</p></div>

<h2>Why women's-specific fit matters</h2>
<p>Women's ski jackets are cut differently from men's and unisex jackets: typically narrower in the shoulders, more room at the chest and hips, shorter sleeves, and a more shaped waist. A jacket that fits well traps warmth better, doesn't ride up when you reach for a pole or a lift bar, and lets you move freely. Unisex jackets work for some bodies, so try both if you can.</p>

<h2>Insulated vs shell</h2>
<h3>Insulated jackets</h3>
<p>Insulated jackets have warmth built in, usually synthetic fill or down. They're the simplest choice if you get cold easily, ski in very cold places, or want one jacket without fussing with layers. The trade-off is that they can get warm on spring days.</p>
<h3>Shell jackets</h3>
<p>A shell is a waterproof outer layer with little or no insulation. You add warmth underneath with a base layer and a fleece or light puffy. Shells are more versatile across temperatures and better for active skiing, hiking, and spring conditions, but you'll need good layers on cold days.</p>
<h3>3-in-1 jackets</h3>
<p>These pair a shell with a zip-in insulated layer you can wear together or separately. They're flexible and good value for occasional skiers, though usually heavier and less refined than separate pieces.</p>
<p>Not sure what goes underneath? See <%= link_to "what to wear skiing", guide_path("what-to-wear-skiing") %>.</p>

<h2>Waterproofing and breathability</h2>
<p>Ski jackets often list two numbers. Waterproof ratings (usually in millimeters) describe how much water pressure the fabric resists; higher numbers hold up better in heavy, wet snow and rain. Breathability ratings (usually in grams) describe how well sweat vapor escapes; higher numbers help if you ski hard or hike. Testing methods vary between brands, so treat the numbers as rough guides rather than exact comparisons.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Dry, cold climates:</strong> moderate waterproofing is usually fine.</li>
  <li><strong>Wet, coastal snow:</strong> choose higher waterproofing and fully taped seams.</li>
  <li><strong>Hard charging or backcountry:</strong> prioritize breathability and venting.</li>
</ul>
<p>Fully taped seams keep water from leaking through stitching; critically taped seams cover only the most exposed areas.</p>

<h2>Length and fit</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Length:</strong> the hem should cover your waistband when you bend forward or sit on a lift. Longer jackets add warmth and protection; shorter ones move more freely.</li>
  <li><strong>Layering room:</strong> try jackets on over the layers you'll actually wear.</li>
  <li><strong>Movement:</strong> raise your arms overhead and reach forward. The sleeves shouldn't pull up past your wrists and the hem shouldn't lift much.</li>
  <li><strong>Hood:</strong> check that it fits over your helmet and turns with your head.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Features worth having</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Powder skirt:</strong> an elastic band inside the hem that keeps snow out when you fall.</li>
  <li><strong>Pit zips:</strong> underarm vents for dumping heat on warm days.</li>
  <li><strong>Wrist gaiters or thumb loops:</strong> seal the gap between sleeve and glove.</li>
  <li><strong>Pass pocket:</strong> a sleeve or chest pocket for an RFID lift pass.</li>
  <li><strong>Interior pockets:</strong> a goggle pocket and a zippered pocket that keeps your phone warm.</li>
  <li><strong>Adjustable cuffs and hem:</strong> cinch out drafts.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Matching jacket to the way you ski</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Resort skiing in cold places:</strong> an insulated jacket with a powder skirt and good hood.</li>
  <li><strong>Varied conditions or spring:</strong> a shell plus layers.</li>
  <li><strong>Wet coastal snow:</strong> high waterproofing, taped seams, and a durable shell.</li>
  <li><strong>Occasional trips:</strong> a 3-in-1 or a mid-weight insulated jacket covers the most situations.</li>
</ul>

